objc 0.2.4-0.2.7 on 32-bit PowerPC (Mac OS X 10.4-10.6).

1. Message sending: objc 0.2 has message-send modules for x86, x86_64, arm and aarch64
only, so the crate doesn't build for powerpc. Add one for 32-bit PowerPC: structures of
every size come back through a hidden pointer (objc_msgSend_stret,
objc_msgSendSuper_stret), integers, pointers and floating-point values in registers
(objc_msgSend, objc_msgSendSuper). objc 0.2 has no type encoding for the return type, so
powerpc.rs tells them apart by size and TypeId; only a structure of 1, 2 or 4 bytes would
be sent the wrong way. cocoa_image (pngquant) only gets pointers and () back.

2. ARC entry points: the 10.4-10.6 libobjc has no objc_retain, objc_release,
objc_autorelease or objc_autoreleasePoolPush/Pop (10.7), so StrongPtr (objc_id::Id,
clipboard 0.5 -> jless) and autoreleasepool failed to link. On powerpc they are Rust
functions that send retain/release/autorelease, [NSAutoreleasePool new] and drain. They
are not exported, so another objc crate in the same program can't clash with them. The
weak-reference functions (objc_initWeak etc.) have no pre-10.7 equivalent and stay
undefined: a program that really uses WeakPtr fails to link instead of misbehaving.

